# MIT red text utilities (`text-mit-red` vs `text-mit-red-ink`)

Source: [`src/styles/mit-theme.css`](src/styles/mit-theme.css) (`--mit-red-core`, `--mit-red-ink`). In **light** mode, `--mit-red-ink` equals `--mit-red-core`, so **`text-mit-red` and `text-mit-red-ink` render the same crimson**. In **`.dark`**, `--mit-red-ink` resolves to **`--foreground`** (high-contrast neutral on the page), while **`text-mit-red`** stays **Institute crimson** on `mit-red-core`.

## Default in TSX

- **Accent links, “view all” / catalog CTAs, breadcrumbs, inline arrows, admin catalog links, and text badges:** use **`text-mit-red dark:text-mit-red-ink`** unless the component already has a stronger contextual dark override such as **`dark:text-white`** or **`dark:has-checked:text-white`**.
- **Do not use bare `text-mit-red` for text.** In dark mode it remains Institute crimson and fails AA contrast on `background`, `card`, `muted`, `mit-surface`, and `mit-red-highlight` surfaces.
- **Do not** use bare **`text-mit-red-ink`** for generic “make it red” links—prefer **`text-mit-red dark:text-mit-red-ink`** so light mode stays brand crimson and dark mode follows the high-contrast foreground ink slot.

## Allowed `text-mit-red-ink` (keep rare and intentional)

1. **Compositional dark variant** next to **`text-mit-red`**, e.g. **`text-mit-red dark:text-mit-red-ink`** in [`siteBrandMitWordmarkDefaultClassName`](src/lib/mit-sailing/tokens.ts), where dark should follow **foreground ink**, not crimson.
2. **Auth inline links:** use the shared constant **`authInlineLinkClassName`** in [`tokens.ts`](src/lib/mit-sailing/tokens.ts)—do not hand-roll duplicate class strings; if the constant changes, one place updates.
3. **CSS / theme only:** `--mit-red-ink` and `--color-mit-red-ink` in `mit-theme.css` stay the semantic name for “ink slot” tied to foreground in dark—do not rename variables in a TSX-only pass.

